  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/timers-and-user-prompts.html#simple-dialogs\r\n\r\n> The alert\\(message\\) method, when invoked, must run the following steps:​\r\n\r\nthe following steps need to apply to alert().\r\n\r\nBut there are none among these steps that explain the conversion of an argument to a string type.\r\nFor example, take an object and take a symbol and try to display both of them via alert.\r\n```\r\nalert({}); /// [object Object]\r\nalert(Symbol()); /// Uncaught TypeError: Cannot convert a Symbol value to a string\r\n```\r\n_Code checked in Google Chrome 77.0.3865.120_\r\nFrom this we can conclude that it is not known how the argument is given in alert.\r\nBecause a symbol can be converted to a string using the usual String constructor\r\n```\r\nString(Symbol()); /// \"Symbol()\"\r\n```\r\nAnd in the first example, alert catches an error, but it is not clear because of what.\r\nIf you still know how the conversion is done, then send me to the address.\r\n\r\nP.S This is probably documented somewhere. I apologize if I am mistaken somewhere.",
